course: world
lesson: 24
title: Groups of animals and how to tell them apart
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Scientists sort animals into groups by their bodies, not by where they live or how they move.
explain: Animals with a backbone are vertebrates: mammals have hair or fur and feed their babies milk; birds have feathers and lay hard-shelled eggs; fish live in water, breathe with gills and most have scales and fins.
explain: Reptiles have dry, scaly skin and most lay leathery eggs on land; amphibians have smooth, moist skin, lay soft eggs in water and usually start life in water before living on land.
explain: Animals without a backbone are invertebrates: insects have six legs and three body parts (head, thorax, abdomen), while spiders have eight legs and two body parts.
explain: A dolphin lives in the sea and has fins, but it breathes air and feeds its baby milk, so it is a mammal, not a fish.
fact: Vertebrates are animals with a backbone.
fact: Mammals have hair or fur and feed their young with milk.
fact: Most mammals give birth to live young.
fact: Birds have feathers, a beak and lay eggs with hard shells.
fact: Fish breathe with gills and most have fins and scales.
fact: Reptiles have dry, scaly skin and most lay eggs with leathery shells.
fact: Amphibians have smooth, moist skin and usually lay their eggs in water.
fact: Young amphibians usually live in water and breathe with gills; adults usually breathe with lungs and through their skin.
fact: Insects have six legs and three body parts: head, thorax and abdomen.
fact: Spiders have eight legs and two body parts, and no antennae.
fact: Insects and spiders have no backbone; they have a hard outer skeleton.
fact: Mammals and birds keep their bodies warm from inside; fish, amphibians and reptiles take their warmth from their surroundings, so people often call them cold-blooded.
fact: Frogs, toads, newts and salamanders are amphibians; snakes, lizards, crocodiles and turtles are reptiles.
fact: Ants, bees, beetles and butterflies are insects; snails, worms, woodlice and scorpions have no backbone either but are not insects: a woodlouse has fourteen legs and a scorpion has eight.
exercise: What do all mammals have? | Hair or fur, and milk for their young | Feathers, a hard beak and two strong wings | Scales and gills | Six legs and wings
exercise: What is special about birds? | They have feathers | They can all fly | They live in trees | They eat seeds
exercise: How do fish breathe? | With gills | With lungs | Through their fins | Through their scales
exercise: Which group has dry, scaly skin and lays leathery eggs on land? | Reptiles | Amphibians | Fish | Mammals
exercise: Which group has smooth, moist skin and lays eggs in water? | Amphibians | Reptiles, like snakes | Birds, like ducks | Insects, like beetles
exercise: How many legs does an insect have? | Six | Eight | Four | Ten
exercise: How many legs does a spider have? | Eight | Only six | Only four | Ten or more
exercise: How many body parts does an insect have? | Three | Only two | Four parts | Just one
exercise: What is a vertebrate? | An animal with a backbone | An animal that lives in water | An animal that has wings | An animal that eats plants
exercise: Which of these is an amphibian? | A frog | A lizard | A snake | A turtle
exercise: Which of these is a reptile? | A crocodile | A salamander | A toad | A newt
exercise: Which of these is an insect? | An ant | A spider | A woodlouse | A scorpion
exercise: Why is a dolphin a mammal and not a fish? | It breathes air and feeds its baby milk | It swims fast and lives all its life in the sea | It has fins and a tail | It eats other fish
exercise: Which animals keep their bodies warm from inside? | Mammals and birds | Fish and reptiles | Amphibians and insects | Reptiles and spiders
exercise: What do insects and spiders have instead of a backbone? | A hard outer skeleton | Soft bones inside | A shell like a snail | Nothing at all to hold them
test: A strange animal has feathers but cannot fly. Which group is it in? | Birds | Mammals | Reptiles | Insects
test: A small animal has eight legs and two body parts. What is it? | A spider | An insect | A beetle | A centipede
test: An animal has smooth, damp skin, lays eggs in a pond and its babies have gills. What is it? | An amphibian | A reptile | A fish | A mammal
test: An animal lives in the sea, has fur and feeds its pups milk. What is it? | A mammal | A fish | An amphibian | A reptile
test: A lizard lies on a warm rock in the morning. Why? | It takes its warmth from its surroundings | It is hiding from the rain | It is looking for water | It is laying its eggs there
test: Which statement is true? | All birds have feathers | All birds can fly | All mammals live on land | All fish lay eggs on land
test: A beetle and a spider are both small. What is one difference? | The beetle has six legs, the spider eight | The beetle has a backbone, the spider not | The spider has wings, the beetle not | The spider has three body parts
test: Which pair are both vertebrates? | A snake and a sparrow | A snail and a shark | A worm and a whale | A bee and a bear
test: An animal has scales, dry skin and lays leathery eggs in sand. What is it? | A reptile | An amphibian | A fish | A bird
test: is a spider an insect or what | No, it has eight legs and two body parts | Yes, all small crawling things are insects | Yes, it has six legs like other insects | No, it is a kind of reptile
test: how can u tell a mammal | It has hair or fur and feeds its young milk | It has feathers and lays eggs | It has scales and breathes with gills | It has six legs and a hard shell
test: Mia reckons frogs are reptiles cos they're cold-blooded. right? | No, frogs are amphibians with smooth, moist skin | Yes, all cold-blooded animals are reptiles | Yes, frogs have dry, scaly skin | No, frogs are fish because they swim
test: my brother says all animals with no backbone are insects. true? | No, spiders and many others have no backbone too | Yes, every animal without a backbone is an insect | Yes, and insects have soft backbones | No, insects do have a backbone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises mammals? | They possess hair or fur and nourish their young with milk | They possess feathers and lay hard-shelled eggs | They possess gills throughout their lives | They possess six legs and three body parts
exam: State the number of legs possessed by an insect. | Six | Eight | Four | Ten
exam: Identify the class of animals possessing smooth, moist skin and usually depositing their eggs in water. | Amphibians | Reptiles | Birds | Mammals
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the bodily warmth of reptiles. | They derive their warmth from their surroundings | They generate their warmth from within | They are warmed by their feathers | They are warmed by a layer of fur
